+    HORIZONTAL chain (head (5,26), reachable rows {20,26,32}) must read [9,14],
+    and the color-15 VERTICAL chain (head (29,2), reachable cols {23,29,35})
+    must read [8,12], a read being the first two rack-block colors coincident
+    with the chain''s segments. The four rack blocks form a cross around center
+    (29,26): 9@(29,14),14@(29,38) on the vertical column; 8@(17,26),12@(41,26)
+    on the horizontal row. Key difficulty: the two chains'' NATURAL readings are
+    exactly SWAPPED from their targets (the vertical column already holds 9,14;
+    the horizontal row already holds 8,12), so the solve is a full CROSSWISE
+    RELOCATION -- move 8,12 onto the vertical column (jammed at the bottom, rows
+    38/44) and 9,14 onto a horizontal row in order. Load-bearing mechanics
+    discovered: (a) extend PUSHES blocks ahead to the far boundary (read when
+    jammed), retract PULLS threaded blocks back toward the head, a
+    wall-braced perpendicular slide CARRIES threaded blocks +/-6 into the
+    adjacent lane but keeps a pair a fixed distance apart (so decoupling needs a
+    core-cell handoff between the two chains); (b) STUCK-CELL INVARIANT: a block
+    whose row is not in {20,26,32} AND whose col is not in {23,29,35} can never
+    be reached by either chain again -- a permanent dead end that the search
+    MUST prune; (c) the vertical read is LOCKABLE (once 8,12 are jammed at rows
+    38/44 the horizontal chain, rows 20/26/32, can never disturb them). Winning
+    line: an 86-action L8 tail after the verified 292-action L1-L7 prefix (378
+    actions total; levels_completed advanced 7->8/WIN on the final action).
